{:edb/db-before-t 1857 :edb/db-after-t 1858 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535170 50 #inst "2026-10-08T18:37:54.754Z" 13194139535170 true] [17592186179503 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/fda8bfb0533b86dc4050d1b8e309e332996b26263b1052d3e251bf0a5c98e816.png)\n\nEvaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0),$ as shown above." 13194139535170 true] [17592186179503 1100 "Evaluate the line integral $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C}12xy\\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the quarter-ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$ traversed from the point $(0,2)$ to the point $(-1,0)$, as shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/68d8dbac3c41c99668f956642b8e26fd.png)" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179503 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2}  = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+4{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3{cos}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 12xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}12xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }24\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=1+3\\cos^2 {t},$ $\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos \n t\\,\\textrm d t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{1+3{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-4{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}({4}^{3/2}-{1}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}(8-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{56}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 true] [17592186179503 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\nx^2 + \\dfrac{y^2}{2^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $1$ and $2$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\big\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: 2\\sin{t} \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-\\sin ti + 2\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-\\sin t)^{2} + (2\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\sin^{2} t + 4\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{3\\cos^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 12xy\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 12 \\cdot \\cos t \\cdot 2\\sin t = 24\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)12xyds &= ∫_{π/2}^{π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{π/2}^{π}24\\sin t\\cos t \\cdot \\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t} \\cdot dt \\\\\n&= 24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution\n\n$$\nu=1+3\\cos^2 {t}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d u=-6\\sin t\\cos t\\,\\textrm d t\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n24∫_{π/2}^{π}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{1 + 3\\cos^{2} t}dt &= -4∫_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{u}du \\\\\n&=-4 \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}u^{3/2} \\mid _{1}^{4} \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(4^{3/2} - 1^{3/2}) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{8}{3}(8 - 1) \\\\\n&=-\\frac{56}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179508 1100 "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s$, where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16$, traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179508 1100 "What expression is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_\\limits{C} \\dfrac{x}{y} \\: \\textrm{d}s,$ where $C$ is the ellipse $x^2 + 16y^2 = 16,$ traversed once in the counterclockwise direction?" 13194139535170 true] [17592186179508 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}=4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\frac{x}{y}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }4\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\sqrt{15{sin}^{2}⁡t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 true] [17592186179508 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{1^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $4$ and $1$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 4\\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad 0 \\le t \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(4\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-4\\sin ti + \\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-4\\sin t)^{2} + (\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + (\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = \\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= \\frac{4\\cos t}{\\sin t} = 4\\cot t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)\\frac{x}{y}ds &= ∫_{0}^{2π}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2π}4\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt \\\\\n&= 4∫_{0}^{2π}\\cot t\\sqrt{15\\sin^{2} t + 1}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179518 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14057969/assets/71df4fc7bd52fe5dd97c7e01502dec2ad68ae31fdf224cccd812c6b7318c70b7.png)\n\nFind, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s,$ where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36,$ shown above." 13194139535170 true] [17592186179518 1100 "Find, as a definite integral, an expression for the line integral of the function $\\displaystyle\\int_\\limits{C} 2xy \\:\\textrm{d}s$, where the curve $C$ is the right half of the ellipse $9x^2 + 4y^2 = 36$, shown above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3690/Source/Images/372d3096b2e6ec488c80ec89998d7543.png)" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179518 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s  = \\int\\limits_a^b  f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3,$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{sin}^{2}⁡t+9{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y) = 2xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}2xy\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}12\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\sqrt{5{cos}^{2}⁡t+4}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 true] [17592186179518 1145 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C f(x, y) \\, \\textrm{d}s = \\int\\limits_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\, \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm{d}t\n$$\n\nNote that we can write the equation of our ellipse in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{2^2} + \\dfrac{y^2}{3^2} = 1\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $2$ and $3$, respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\cos{t}, \\: 3\\sin{t} \\right\\rangle, \\qquad -\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\le t \\le \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nComputing $\\mathbf{r}'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf{r}'(t) \\|$, we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{′}(t) &= \\frac{dx}{dt}i + \\frac{dy}{dt}j \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dt}(2\\cos t)i + \\frac{d}{dt}(3\\sin t)j \\\\\n&=-2\\sin ti + 3\\cos tj \\\\\n‖r^{′}(t)‖ &= \\sqrt{(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-2\\sin t)^{2} + (3\\cos t)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4\\sin^{2} t + 9\\cos^{2} t} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4(\\sin^{2} t + \\cos^{2} t)} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = 2xy\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(r(t)) &= 2(2\\cos t)(3\\sin t) \\\\\n&= 12\\sin t\\cos t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∫_(C)2xyds &= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}f(r(t))‖r^{′}(t)‖dt \\\\\n&= ∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}12\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt \\\\\n&= 12∫_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\sin t\\cos t\\sqrt{5\\cos^{2} t + 4}dt\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535170 false] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335241 13194139535170 true] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335248 13194139535170 true] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335255 13194139535170 true] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335269 13194139535170 true] [17592186179641 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335276 13194139535170 true] [17592186179646 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335206 13194139535170 true] [17592186179646 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335213 13194139535170 true] [17592186179646 1020 #edb/ref 17592186335220 13194139535170 true] [17592186179646 1020 #edb/